body:
Acceptance for the scan-optimisation wins is exercised ONLY on SELF-CONTAINED synthetic scratch fixtures that the tests build themselves under the system temp dir (/tmp), per global.constraint.no-real-project-test. No test reads any external workload checkout — there is no ~/jgrapht, no ~/netbeans, and no env-var-resolved reference tree as an acceptance input — and NO recorded external workload baseline (wall time, per-phase split, node/edge counts) applies as an acceptance bound. Every acceptance bound is stated relative to a full scan of the SAME synthetic fixture, measured in the same run.

Acceptance shape — three behavioural scenarios:
(1) a fresh, unchanged worktree re-scan is near-instant: it takes the freshness fast path (no frontend spawn, no DB rebuild);
(2) a small (1–5 file) localized edit re-scans on the incremental/splice path (not a full rebuild) and finishes materially below a full scan of the same fixture; its graph equals a from-scratch full scan of the same changed tree (same node set, same edge set, same unresolved targets);
(3) a signature change to a widely-depended-on symbol may legitimately approach a full scan, but must stay exact: the incremental graph equals a from-scratch full scan of the same changed tree (same node set, same edge set, same unresolved targets).

AC: the three scenarios are exercised on self-contained synthetic scratch fixtures (no external checkout is read) and each is measured — and its bound asserted relative to the same-fixture full scan — with the per-phase timing report (frontend / ingest-assembly / DB-load / startup-overhead). No acceptance bound is stated as an external-workload number and no external baseline is required.
